We ask whether intraday price moves beyond an implied-volatility&nbsp; 
<br>
 band in E-mini Nasdaq-100 futures (NQ) revert before the session&nbsp; 
<br>
 closes. The band is set daily as ± VXN/16 relative to the&nbsp; 
<br>
 prior-session close; dividing by 16 converts the annualised VXN&nbsp; 
<br>
 to a one-day standard-deviation estimate. Over 860 algorithmically&nbsp; 
<br>
 detected band-breach events (2018–2026), the unconditional&nbsp; 
<br>
 same-session reversion rate is 85.2%. When we sort by VXN level,&nbsp; 
<br>
 the Sharpe–VXN profile is non-monotone. Reversion probability is&nbsp; 
<br>
 statistically significant in all four VXN buckets (BH-adjusted&nbsp; 
<br>
 p &lt; 0.001), but the 30-minute exit rule generates positive Sharpe&nbsp; 
<br>
 only at the extremes (VXN &lt; 20: 0.38; VXN &gt;= 30: 0.64), with&nbsp; 
<br>
 slightly negative values in between. Walk-forward validation&nbsp; 
<br>
 confirms the pattern out of sample (IS 2018–2022, n = 298,&nbsp; 
<br>
 Sharpe = 0.47; OOS 2023–2026, n = 191, Sharpe = 1.29). A&nbsp; 
<br>
 pre-sample from 2010–2017 gives a Sharpe of -0.94 without the&nbsp; 
<br>
 regime filter. A momentum alternative that enters in the breach&nbsp; 
<br>
 direction is rejected on the same data. The band effect depends&nbsp; 
<br>
 on volatility, with the strongest edge appearing when VXN &gt;= 30.

As climate change continues to cause disproportionate harms in regions that have done little to contribute to emissions, are limited in their capacity to affect the global emissions trajectory, and lack the resources to adapt to its devastating effects, compensation for loss and damages (L&amp;D) has emerged as a crucial pillar for an equitable international climate policy response. L&amp;D refers to the residual climate change damages that will be experienced despite mitigation and adaptation policy because of the shift to a warmer world. In this Article, I combine a first-principles-based conceptual framework that captures the key normative concerns animating countries in international negotiations with an empirically based numerical model to produce estimates for L&amp;D compensation.&nbsp;&nbsp; 
<br>
 Depending on the assumed “start year” for damages (prospective versus retrospective approach) and the principle for allocating L&amp;D compensation responsibilities (Excused Ignorance, Strict Liability, Ability to Pay), the L&amp;D fund size ranges from ~$150B - $480B in the near term and grows to ~$9.5T - $25T by the end of the century. Compared to present day commitments of $661M at United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), modeled results from this Article suggest a significant scale up in ambition is needed to match the scope of expected L&amp;D in the coming decades.&nbsp; 
<br>
 The US, Japan, Russia and several countries in Europe are net contributors for all modeled frameworks, whereas India and countries in Africa are always net beneficiaries of an L&amp;D compensation scheme. For other regions, the magnitude and direction of L&amp;D compensation depends on the normative principle endorsed. For instance, China is a net payer for L&amp;D compensation in the Excused Ignorance scenario, but a net recipient in the Strict Liability and Ability to Pay scenarios. These differences highlight the stakes of the normative disagreements for allocating compensation responsibility even when carbon taxation is set at globally efficient levels.&nbsp; 
<br>
 From an implementation perspective, most regions with L&amp;D payment obligations can meet their responsibilities through global recycling of regional carbon tax revenues in the near-term. By the end of the century, however, L&amp;D compensation will require additional sources of climate finance, imposing additional fiscal costs on economies.
